From: NixOS for nabu
Subject: [PATCH] serial: qcom-geni: force runtime suspend during system sleep

During system sleep the PM core's prepare phase pins a runtime PM
reference on every device (device_prepare() -> pm_runtime_get_noresume(),
"block runtime suspend here, during the prepare phase"), which is only
dropped again in device_complete().  qcom_geni_serial_suspend() relies on
uart_suspend_port() -> uart_change_pm(UART_PM_STATE_OFF) ->
pm_runtime_put_sync() to reach the runtime-suspend callback, but that put
can only bring the usage count back down to the reference held by the PM
core.  qcom_geni_serial_runtime_suspend() therefore never runs for a port
that is kept open across system sleep (e.g. a Bluetooth serdev), so
geni_serial_resources_off() -> geni_se_resources_off() never runs either.

Besides leaving the SE clocks and interconnect enabled, that skips
pinctrl_pm_select_sleep_state(), so the QUP pads stay muxed to their
peripheral function with bias-disable instead of being switched to the
"sleep" pinctrl state (GPIO input, RX pull-up) that boards such as the
Xiaomi Pad 5 (sm8150/nabu) provide for their Bluetooth UART.  The result
is a floating/noisy RX pad and a TLMM wake-IRQ status that is latched
while the dedicated wake IRQ is masked; the PM core then arms that wake
IRQ in dpm_suspend_noirq and the latched edge fires immediately, so the
system resumes from suspend-to-idle right away.

Fix this by calling pm_runtime_force_suspend() at the end of
qcom_geni_serial_suspend() and pm_runtime_force_resume() at the start of
qcom_geni_serial_resume(), so the runtime-suspend callback always runs
regardless of the usage count.

Backport of upstream d0cd9c8d0fd59bc7d140f3d60cf02e1d80376dab
("serial: qcom-geni: add force suspend/resume to system sleep
callbacks"), which is in tty-next but not in 6.17.y.  The upstream patch
also warns about a runtime_error; that part is a later refactoring and is
not required here.

diff --git a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -2018,6 +2018,7 @@
 	struct qcom_geni_serial_port *port = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
+	int ret;
 
 	/*
 	 * This is done so we can hit the lowest possible state in suspend
@@ -2027,7 +2028,30 @@
 		geni_icc_set_tag(&port->se, QCOM_ICC_TAG_ACTIVE_ONLY);
 		geni_icc_set_bw(&port->se);
 	}
-	return uart_suspend_port(private_data->drv, uport);
+
+	ret = uart_suspend_port(private_data->drv, uport);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * uart_suspend_port() only drops the runtime PM reference that
+	 * uart_startup() took. During system sleep the PM core holds its own
+	 * reference from device_prepare(), so the usage count never reaches
+	 * zero here and qcom_geni_serial_runtime_suspend() is not invoked.
+	 * That leaves the SE clocks/interconnect enabled and, more
+	 * importantly, skips pinctrl_pm_select_sleep_state() in
+	 * geni_se_resources_off(), so the pads stay in their "default" state
+	 * instead of the "sleep" state. Force the runtime suspend so the
+	 * resources are really gated and the sleep pinctrl (GPIO mode with
+	 * the RX pull-up) is applied before the wake IRQ gets armed.
+	 *
+	 * When no_console_suspend is set the console must remain active
+	 * across system sleep, so skip the force suspend path.
+	 */
+	if (!console_suspend_enabled && uart_console(uport))
+		return 0;
+
+	return pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
 }
 
 static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
@@ -2037,6 +2061,10 @@
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
 
+	ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	ret = uart_resume_port(private_data->drv, uport);
 	if (uart_console(uport)) {
 		geni_icc_set_tag(&port->se, QCOM_ICC_TAG_ALWAYS);
